    "body": "<p>On warm nights near ponds and wetlands, male frogs and toads gather and call in choruses that can be heard hundreds of metres away. Each species has a characteristic call: a trill, a peep, a banjo-like pluck, or a deep croak, produced by passing air over vocal cords and amplifying it with a vocal sac that inflates like a balloon. Calling is the main way males attract females, and females choose partly by call features such as pulse rate and dominant frequency, which correlate with body size and condition. Choruses are competitive and cooperative at once: males space themselves and alternate calls to avoid masking one another, yet the combined chorus also helps females find the breeding site and may reduce each individual&#x27;s predation risk. Because amphibians are sensitive to water quality, disease, and climate, a thinning frog chorus is treated by ecologists as an early warning signal for wetland health.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: amphibian calls are species-specific and are the standard identification tool for field surveys: trained listeners and automated recorders identify species from call structure alone. Experiments that play back altered calls show females discriminating by pulse rate and frequency, confirming that call features carry mate-choice information. Long-term acoustic monitoring programmes track calling activity across seasons to detect population declines, including declines linked to chytrid fungus disease.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if a chorus holds 60 calling males and each call lasts 0.5 seconds repeated every 2.0 seconds, each male&#x27;s duty cycle is 0.5 / 2.0 = 25.0 percent, and the chorus produces 60 / 2.0 = 30.0 calls per second in total. The demo prints duty cycle 25.0 percent, calls per second 30.0, and calls per minute 1800.0.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published amphibian bioacoustics literature and wetland monitoring programme summaries; call structure and mate-choice findings as published October 2026</p>",
